# Hermetic llama-server.log slice for the release->backend-cancel joiner
# contract (LP-0MVW8S6SR003MBP5). Covers the same fake window as
# abandoned_prefill_release_cancel_proxy.log; lines carry no wall-clock
# timestamp (real llama-server behaviour), so the analyser is only valid when
# this file covers exactly the analysed window.
#
# Real line shapes copied from /var/log/llama-proxy/llama-server.log*:
#   [pid] slot launch_slot_: id  S | task -1 | sampler chain: ...
#   [pid] slot launch_slot_: id  S | task T | processing task, is_child = 0
#   [pid] slot update_slots: id  S | task T | prompt processing progress, n_tokens = N, batch.n_tokens = B, progress = P
#   [pid] srv          stop: cancel task, id_task = T
#   [pid] slot      release: id  S | task T | stop processing: n_tokens = N, truncated = 0
#   [pid] prompt eval time =   <ms> ms / <tokens> tokens ( ... )
#   srv    operator(): http client error: Connection handling canceled     <-- no pid, no task id
#
# Ground truth (one segment per release, in release order):
#   Segment A (R_a -> task 41): launch -> 2 progress -> CANCEL -> 2 progress
#       (n_tokens 40000 -> 50000, so 10000 tokens after the cancel) -> stop processing.
#   Segment B (R_b -> task 43): launch -> 1 progress -> stop processing. NO cancel
#       anywhere in this segment: a naive time-window join must not fabricate one.
#   Segment C (R_c): a bare operator() http client error: Connection handling
#       canceled line - today's cancel shape carries no task id, so the release
#       is unjoined with a machine-readable reason.
#   Segment D (R_d -> task 42): launch -> 2 progress -> CANCEL -> prompt eval
#       time (the cancelled task still emitted an eval) -> immediate stop
#       processing (0 tokens evaluated after the cancel => 0.0 s latency).
